  "image_name": "2b6ece34ea9f4ebee27db1d577221d1e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how gut microbes can influence intestinal pain through several epithelial and neuroimmune routes. Across the top, the microbiota is shown above the intestinal epithelium, with one group of bacteria highlighted as *E. coli*. On the left, antimicrobial peptides such as lysozyme, normally released by Paneth cells, appear to restrain bacteria; the crossed-out Paneth cell suggests loss or dysfunction of this defense, allowing bacterial effects that activate nearby nociceptor endings and increase pain.\n\nIn the center, microbial metabolites, labeled isovalerate, are depicted acting on the epithelium and contributing to signaling toward sensory nerves. A prominent pathway involves enterochromaffin cells, which release serotonin onto a receptor labeled 5-HT3R on afferent nerve terminals, again linked to increased pain. On the right, a goblet cell is shown producing mucus and releasing CGRP near another nerve branch, implying that epithelial secretory cells also communicate with sensory neurons.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that the intestinal microbiota does not affect pain only indirectly through inflammation; it can act through epithelial cell types, antimicrobial defenses, metabolites, and neurotransmitter-like mediators to modulate nociceptor activity and heighten visceral pain.",
